{"parentUuid":"9b1f529f-640f-4698-8274-a69199b46d96","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"44611460-2829-462e-b2e9-af8e653be0db","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Base directory for this skill: C:\\Users\\khair\\.claude\\skills\\windows-computer-use\n\n<purpose>\r\nGive an agent full, faithful control of the local Windows desktop — anything a\r\nuser can do with mouse, keyboard, and screen — through one deterministic CLI.\r\nUI Automation (UIA) is primary: enumerate windows, read element trees as text,\r\nand target controls by AutomationId / Name / ControlType. Screenshots + absolute\r\ncoordinate input are the vision fallback for surfaces UIA cannot see.\r\n</purpose>\r\n\r\n<constraints>\r\n- WINDOWS ONLY. Must run in the user's interactive session (not a headless service). uv auto-installs deps from the script's PEP-723 header.\r\n- UIA-FIRST, VISION FALLBACK. Resolve targets by AutomationId → Name+ControlType → path. There is NO automatic CLI fallback: when the CLI returns `target_not_found` on a canvas / custom-drawn surface (or some Electron / games), the ORCHESTRATOR explicitly switches to a screenshot + `--at x,y` coordinate click. The CLI never silently downgrades from UIA to pixels.\r\n- COORDINATES ARE VIRTUAL-DESKTOP SPACE. Primary monitor top-left = (0,0); monitors left/above are negative. Mouse input uses SendInput ABSOLUTE|VIRTUALDESK so clicks land correctly across mixed-DPI monitors. The process is Per-Monitor-V2 DPI aware.\r\n- INPUT IS A GLOBAL SERIAL RESOURCE. One mouse, one keyboard, one foreground window. Exactly ONE action at a time across the whole machine. Read-only inspection (tree/find/screenshot/windows/monitors) is safe to run concurrently.\r\n- FAIL FAST AND LOUD. Every command prints one JSON object: success → stdout `{\"ok\":true,...}`; failure → stderr `{\"ok\":false,\"error\":...,\"reason\":...}` + non-zero exit. Never guess past ambiguity.\r\n- BROWSER WORK → use chrome-control / browser skills, not this skill. Only drive a browser here as a last resort when those are unavailable.\r\n</constraints>\r\n\r\n<orchestration>\r\nRun this skill in a fork-orchestrated loop so heavy output (UIA trees, screenshots) never lands in the main context.\r\n\r\n- MAIN (orchestrator): holds the objective, a rolling sub-goal plan, and a running model of UI state built from fork reports. NEVER calls win_use.py directly; never holds a tree or screenshot. Decomposes the objective into coherent sub-goals, dispatches them, reads compact reports, re-plans from what actually happened.\r\n- ACTION FORK (exactly one, persistent): owns the perceive→act loop for one sub-goal at a time. Main keeps feeding it successive sub-goals. All trees/screenshots stay in its context; it returns only a compact report. RECYCLE it when switching to a different app/window (clean re-orientation boundary) or after a long run of sub-goals.\r\n- PERCEPTION FORKS (many, parallel, read-only): inspect background windows, verify a result, or describe the screen. Safe to fan out because they never touch mouse/keyboard/focus.\r\n\r\nSpawn forks with the Agent tool (`subagent_type: \"fork\"`) so they inherit main's context + prompt cache; address the persistent action fork via SendMessage.\r\n</orchestration>\r\n\r\n<fork_contract>\r\nThe action fork is a FAITHFUL EXECUTOR, not a problem-solver.\r\n\r\n- Execute the instructed sub-goal exactly. Handle normal UI flow on its own: dismiss unexpected non-destructive dialogs, retry an action that had no visible effect, proceed through auth/login prompts.\r\n- STOP AND REPORT (do not improvise a workaround) on either trigger:\r\n  1. TARGET NOT FOUND / AMBIGUOUS — the instructed element is missing, or multiple match and it cannot disambiguate (CLI returns reason `target_not_found` or `ambiguous`).\r\n  2. UNEXPECTED IRREVERSIBLE ACTION — a Delete / Send / Overwrite / Discard / Empty / Format / Uninstall / Pay confirmation that is NOT listed in the sub-goal's `sanctioned_irreversible`. The FORK (the LLM) detects this itself by inspecting the UIA tree BEFORE clicking a Delete/Send/etc. button — read the dialog/button text from the tree and decide. The CLI does NOT enforce this; it only provides the inspect + click primitives. Irreversible actions the sub-goal explicitly sanctioned are carried out.\r\n- Log every action (the CLI writes the action log automatically).\r\n\r\nON A FORK STOP: main spawns a read-only perception fork to diagnose the real screen state, then main decides — correct the sub-goal and re-dispatch (find another solution), OR escalate to the user via AskUser with a cropped screenshot (SendUserFile). Forks never improvise; main may.\r\n</fork_contract>\r\n\r\n<workflow>\r\n1. Main: read the objective; decompose into the next coherent sub-goal with its `sanctioned_irreversible` list.\r\n2. Action fork: orient — `windows` then `tree`/`find` on the target window (text, cheap). Use a screenshot only when UIA is blind.\r\n3. Action fork: act via the smallest reliable primitive (UIA invoke or real SendInput click/type/key), verifying the resulting state from the UIA tree, not pixels.\r\n4. Action fork: on success return a compact report; on a stop trigger return the blocker + small evidence (cropped element screenshot or trimmed tree slice).\r\n5. Main: update its UI-state model, decide the next sub-goal or escalate, recycle the action fork on app/window switch.\r\n</workflow>\r\n\r\n<scripts>\r\nAll commands (quote the path — repo path may contain spaces):\r\n  uv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/windows-computer-use/scripts/win_use.py\" <command> [options]\r\n\r\nGlobal options: `--log <path>` action log (JSONL, default `WIN_USE_LOG` env or `./tmp/win_use_actions.jsonl`); `--daemon` / `WIN_USE_DAEMON=1` route this call to a running daemon; `--host` / `--port` / `--port-file` locate it.\r\n\r\nRead-only (parallel-safe):\r\n- `monitors` — monitors (rect, size, work area, dpi, scale, primary) + virtual-screen bounds + dpi mode.\r\n- `windows [--all]` — top-level windows: hwnd, title, class, pid, rect, visible, minimized, monitor, z-order.\r\n- `tree --window <hwnd|title> [--depth 14] [--max-nodes 4000]` — UIA element tree (control_type, name, automation_id, class, rect, enabled/offscreen when notable).\r\n- `find --window <w> [--name S] [--control T] [--automation-id ID] [--limit 50]` — matching elements with rect + `path`.\r\n- `screenshot --out <path> [--monitor N | --window <w> | --region x,y,w,h]` — PNG. Default = whole virtual desktop. `--monitor` index is from `monitors`.\r\n\r\nActions (serial — one at a time):\r\n- `focus --window <w>` — restore if minimized + bring to foreground.\r\n- `click [--window <w> (--name|--control|--automation-id) [--index N] | --at x,y] [--button left|right|middle] [--double] [--method input|invoke]` — `input` = real SendInput mouse to element center (default, most faithful); `invoke` = UIA InvokePattern (windowless).\r\n- `type --text \"<t>\" [--window <w> [(--name|--control|--automation-id) [--index N]]] [--method keys|setvalue]` — with a target it clicks to focus then types; `setvalue` uses UIA ValuePattern. Literal text is auto-escaped.\r\n- `key (--combo \"ctrl+shift+s\" | --raw \"<send_keys>\") [--window <w>]` — combos accept ctrl/alt/shift/win + named keys (enter, esc, tab, f1–f24, up…) ; `--raw` passes pywinauto send_keys syntax.\r\n- `scroll [--window <w> (--name|--control|--automation-id) | --at x,y] --amount N [--horizontal]` — wheel notches (+up / −down).\r\n- `drag --start x,y --end x,y [--button left|right|middle]` — press-move-release in virtual-desktop coords.\r\n- `wait --window <w> [--name|--control|--automation-id] [--timeout 10] [--poll 0.3]` — poll until an element appears.\r\n\r\nDaemon (optional — keeps UIA/COM warm, ~halves per-call latency by skipping init):\r\n- `[--port N] daemon` — start the daemon (blocks; run it in the background). Default `--port 0` = OS-assigned; writes a port-file (`./tmp/win_use_daemon.json`) with the actual port + pid and warms UIA on start. `--host` is LOOPBACK-ONLY (`127.0.0.1` / `localhost` / `::1`); any other host fails loud (`reason: unsafe_host`). A second daemon on the same port-file refuses to start (`reason: daemon_already_running`).\r\n- `daemon --status` / `daemon --stop` — check or cleanly stop a running daemon. UIA/command work runs on ONE dedicated worker thread while the accept loop answers control messages itself, so `--status` / `--stop` stay responsive even while a long command (e.g. `wait`) is in flight.\r\n- Route any command through it: `--daemon <command …>` (or set `WIN_USE_DAEMON=1` once in a fork so every call routes; locate the daemon with `WIN_USE_DAEMON_PORT` or `--port` / `--port-file`). The daemon serialises all UIA/command work on its one COM-safe worker thread — which also enforces one-action-at-a-time. If routing is requested but no daemon is reachable, the call fails loud (`reason: no_daemon` / `daemon_unreachable`).\r\n- Pass ABSOLUTE paths for `--out` when routed: the daemon resolves relative paths against its OWN working directory (the orchestrator absolutizes `--out` against the client cwd before forwarding). `--log` is forwarded automatically.\r\n- WINDOWS env vars: set them in PowerShell with `$env:WIN_USE_DAEMON=1` / `$env:WIN_USE_DAEMON_PORT=53999` (the inline `VAR=val cmd` form is bash-only).\r\n</scripts>\r\n\r\n<inputs>\r\nSub-goal (main → action fork), conceptual shape:\r\n- `objective`: one coherent task ending at a stable, verifiable UI state (e.g. \"fill and submit the login form in window 'X'\").\r\n- `target_hints`: window title/hwnd and any known AutomationId / Name to aim at.\r\n- `sanctioned_irreversible`: an orchestrator → fork sub-goal field (NOT a CLI flag) — the explicit list of irreversible confirmations this sub-goal may push through (e.g. `[\"delete file report.csv\"]`). The fork (LLM) checks each Delete/Send/Overwrite/etc. dialog it sees in the UIA tree against this list; anything irreversible NOT listed → stop. The CLI is unaware of it.\r\n- `success_criteria`: the observable UIA state that means done.\r\n</inputs>\r\n\r\n<outputs>\r\nReport (action fork → main), compact:\r\n- `outcome`: success | partial | failed | stopped.\r\n- `actions`: resolved targets acted on (by Name/AutomationId, not raw pixels).\r\n- `final_state`: the 2–3 UIA facts main cares about (not the whole tree).\r\n- on stop: `reason` — either a CLI reason (`target_not_found` | `ambiguous`) or a fork-raised `unexpected_irreversible` (the LLM's own decision from reading the dialog text in the UIA tree; not a CLI code) — plus small evidence (cropped element screenshot path or trimmed tree slice).\r\n\r\nAction log: append-only JSONL at `--log` path; one record per action with timestamp, resolved target, method, and result. The audit trail — pass a consistent `--log` across a session.\r\n</outputs>\r\n\r\n<validation>\r\n- Before acting, resolve the target; a single unambiguous element must match, else the CLI returns `ambiguous` (with all candidates) or `target_not_found` — both are stop triggers, exit 1.\r\n- Verify outcomes from the UIA tree / element state, not from the screenshot, whenever a structured value exists (e.g. a display/value/toggle state).\r\n- Treat any non-zero exit / `{\"ok\":false}` as a hard signal; read `reason` and report up rather than retrying blindly.\r\n- Disambiguate `ambiguous` with `--index N` (or a more specific Name/AutomationId) only when main/the plan knows which one is correct.\r\n- CLI `reason` codes you may see: `target_not_found`, `ambiguous`, `index_out_of_range`, `stale_target` (UI changed mid-resolution), `invoke_failed`, `setvalue_failed`, `setvalue_needs_target` (setvalue with no element target), `focus_denied` (could not foreground the window), `window_not_found`, `window_ambiguous` (title matched >1 window), `window_minimized` (screenshot a restored window first), `wait_timeout`, `usage_error` (bad CLI args), `unsupported_platform` (not Windows), and daemon codes `no_daemon`, `daemon_unreachable`, `daemon_protocol`, `daemon_already_running`, `unsafe_host`, `message_too_large`.\r\n</validation>\r\n\r\n<limits>\r\nHard OS walls (not bugs — report and escalate, don't fight):\r\n- UAC secure desktop (elevation prompts) and the lock screen are off-limits — UIA and SendInput cannot cross them.\r\n- A non-elevated process cannot send input to elevated (admin) windows (UIPI). To drive elevated apps, run win_use.py elevated.\r\n- Games / DirectX exclusive-fullscreen expose no UIA and may capture black via GDI — best-effort only.\r\n- UIA on Electron/Qt/Java apps can be shallow or absent; fall back to screenshot + `--at`.\r\n</limits>\r\n\r\n<examples>\r\n- Map the screen:    uv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/windows-computer-use/scripts/win_use.py\" monitors\r\n- List windows:      uv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/windows-computer-use/scripts/win_use.py\" windows\r\n- Read a window:     uv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/windows-computer-use/scripts/win_use.py\" tree --window \"Settings\"\r\n- Find a control:    uv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/windows-computer-use/scripts/win_use.py\" find --window \"Settings\" --control Button --name \"Bluetooth\"\r\n- Click it:          uv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/windows-computer-use/scripts/win_use.py\" click --window \"Settings\" --automation-id \"BluetoothToggle\"\r\n- Type into a field: uv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/windows-computer-use/scripts/win_use.py\" type --window \"Run\" --text \"notepad\"\r\n- Key chord:         uv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/windows-computer-use/scripts/win_use.py\" key --combo \"ctrl+s\"\r\n- Screenshot screen: uv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/windows-computer-use/scripts/win_use.py\" screenshot --monitor 0 --out tmp/shots/screen.png\r\n- Vision fallback:   uv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/windows-computer-use/scripts/win_use.py\" click --at 1493,909\r\n- Start daemon (bg): uv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/windows-computer-use/scripts/win_use.py\" --port 53999 daemon   # run in background\r\n- Routed call (PS):  $env:WIN_USE_DAEMON=1; $env:WIN_USE_DAEMON_PORT=53999; uv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/windows-computer-use/scripts/win_use.py\" tree --window \"Settings\"\r\n</examples>\r\n\r\n<notes>\r\n- Runtimes: uv + Python ≥3.10. Deps (pywinauto, mss, pillow) install automatically from the PEP-723 header on first run.\r\n- `--method invoke` is windowless and does not move the mouse — use it for reliable button presses; default `input` performs a real mouse click to the element center, which is the most faithful \"as a user\" path and is required for controls without an InvokePattern.\r\n- Screenshots are large (image tokens) — capture the smallest useful region (an element rect or single monitor), and prefer reading the UIA tree (text) over screenshots wherever possible.\r\n- For a multi-call session, start the daemon ONCE (orchestrator or action fork, in the background) and set `$env:WIN_USE_DAEMON=1` + `$env:WIN_USE_DAEMON_PORT` (PowerShell) so every fork's calls route through the one warm process — which also serialises input, reinforcing the one-action-at-a-time invariant. Stop it with `daemon --stop` when done.\r\n- For controlling Chrome / any web browser, use chrome-control and the browser skills — they are more robust than driving a browser's UI through UIA here.\r\n</notes>\r\n\n\nARGUMENTS: Read-only verify the restarted installed Superset desktop app is visibly running and not blank. Inspect the exact Superset window, capture a screenshot under tmp/, and report the visible route/workspace without clicking or changing state. Do not expose terminal/transcript content in the report or screenshot beyond what is necessary; crop to app chrome if possible."}]},"isMeta":true,"uuid":"f3e4ca2f-d946-42f7-ad5d-6895c112a145","timestamp":"2026-08-12T02:13:50.734Z","sourceToolUseID":"call_pOjtrfKnHQ4LxRTGglQKQW83","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
