{"parentUuid":"dc96910b-06aa-46fe-a530-4d69f0c98e3b","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"44611460-2829-462e-b2e9-af8e653be0db","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_PuZ7tH8JT31FqD4zM5Ob42ad","type":"tool_result","content":"90\t * bridge that refused every answer the day after a Claude Code auto-update would\n91\t * be a worse failure than the drift it was warning about.\n92\t *\n93\t * DO NOT advance this constant on the strength of reading the CLI's source. The\n94\t * BYTES — that one bare digit still selects and submits, that digit N+1 opens the\n95\t * editor, that `\\r` submits it — are behaviour, and only a live pty shows\n96\t * behaviour. It stands at 2.1.226 because all three were DRIVEN there and the\n97\t * agent's own `tool_result` confirmed each one; source reading alone would never\n98\t * have earned it. `RENDER_OBSERVED_AGAINST` below is the weaker claim that source\n99\t * reading does support, kept separate so nothing can quietly upgrade one into the\n100\t * other.\n101\t */\n102\texport const PROVEN_AGAINST = \"claude-code@2.1.226\";\n103\t\n104\t/**\n105\t * The build whose RENDERED SHAPE has been observed directly: the free-text labels\n106\t * below, and the row order `[...options, freeText, chat?]` with the chat row\n107\t * carrying the sentinel `\"__chat__\"`.\n108\t *\n109\t * REFUTED by a live pty run, and corrected here rather than left as folklore: a\n110\t * multi-select picker DOES render a free-text row at N+1 and the chat row at N+2.\n111\t * The binary's `!mL.multiSelect` gate does not mean what the earlier comment\n112\t * claimed. What that row does when its digit is pressed was then driven, twice,\n113\t * and the answer is worse than \"unknown\" — see `multiSelectFreeTextBytesProven`.\n114\t * Chat-row context-gating is still unconfirmed, so nothing asserts its presence\n115\t * OR its absence.\n116\t *\n117\t * Strictly weaker than `PROVEN_AGAINST` and never a substitute for it. It exists\n118\t * so the copy constants can cite what they are true of without implying the byte\n119\t * contract was re-driven.\n120\t */\n121\texport const RENDER_OBSERVED_AGAINST = \"claude-code@2.1.226\";\n122\t\n123\t/**\n124\t * (PICKER-CONTRACT-VERSIONED) One entry per Claude Code build the fork has\n125\t * OBSERVED, owning both halves of the contract for that build: the bytes, and the\n126\t * exact copy the free-text row renders.\n127\t *\n128\t * The two halves have to live together and be keyed by version, because they were\n129\t * briefly not: the free-text labels pinned 2.1.226's \"Type something.\" while\n130\t * `PROVEN_AGAINST` declared 2.1.220, whose row reads \"Other\". One of the two was\n131\t * always going to be false, and on the build whose bytes the fork claims to drive\n132\t * the row would never have been found at all.\n133\t *\n134\t * The `*BytesProven` flags are PER SHAPE and per version, because the shapes are\n135\t * genuinely independent behaviours of the same row: on 2.1.226 the one-question\n136\t * and N>1 free-text sequences both work and the multi-select one is actively\n137\t * harmful (see each flag). 2.1.220 is recorded as it was driven at the time —\n138\t * only the one-question shape — and is NOT retro-fitted with claims about a build\n139\t * nobody re-drove.\n140\t *\n141\t * An UNKNOWN version resolves to `null` and everything downstream fails closed.\n142\t * To add a build: append an entry with what a pty run actually showed, and move\n143\t * `PROVEN_AGAINST` only if its bytes were re-driven.\n144\t */\n145\texport interface PickerContract {\n146\t\tversion: string;\n147\t\t/**\n148\t\t * The label the free-text row renders, exactly, on a SINGLE-SELECT question.\n149\t\t * A multi-select question renders different copy (2.1.226: \"Type something\",\n150\t\t * no full stop) and a checkbox before it, which is deliberately not recorded\n151\t\t * as a usable label — no multi-select free-text row is ever pressed.\n152\t\t */\n153\t\tfreeTextRowLabel: string;\n154\t\t/**\n155\t\t * Whether `[digit N+1, text, \"\\r\"]` was driven on a prompt of exactly ONE\n156\t\t * single-select question, where the `\\r` submits the whole prompt.\n157\t\t */\n158\t\tfreeTextOneBytesProven: boolean;\n159\t\t/**\n160\t\t * (FREETEXT-N2-PROVEN) Whether the SAME three keystrokes were driven for a\n161\t\t * question inside an N > 1 single-select prompt, where the `\\r` advances to\n162\t\t * the next question and the prompt is submitted by the review-screen return.\n163\t\t */\n164\t\tfreeTextManyBytesProven: boolean;\n165\t\t/**\n166\t\t * (MSEL-N2-PROVEN) Whether `[toggle digit × k, \"\\x1b[C\"]` was driven as a\n167\t\t * per-question group for a multi-select question INSIDE an N > 1 prompt —\n168\t\t * the arrow advancing to the next question mid-prompt and onto the review\n169\t\t * screen on the last, with the review-screen `\\r` submitting.\n170\t\t */\n171\t\tmultiSelectManyBytesProven: boolean;\n172\t\t/**\n173\t\t * Whether free text on a MULTI-SELECT question has a proven sequence.\n174\t\t *\n175\t\t * `false` on every build, and on 2.1.226 that is a REFUTATION rather than an\n176\t\t * absence of evidence — see the refusal in `validateAnswerItem`.\n177\t\t */\n178\t\tmultiSelectFreeTextBytesProven: boolean;\n179\t\t/**\n180\t\t * (GUARD5-MSEL-EDITOR-DETECT) DETECTION-ONLY: the exact raw-row copy of the\n181\t\t * multi-select editor row in each toggle state, as the build renders it at\n182\t\t * digit `options.length + 1`. Guard 5 uses it as numbering evidence — the\n183\t\t * row renders BELOW every option, so seeing it while options are missing\n184\t\t * proves a mis-numbered screen rather than a clip. It is NEVER offered as a\n185\t\t * pressable slot (`provenFreeTextOption` refuses multi-select free text;\n186\t\t * on 2.1.226 the press is refuted, not unproven). Empty when the copy was\n187\t\t * never captured for the build — the contradiction check then simply stays\n188\t\t * unarmed for multi items, exactly as it was before the needle existed.\n189\t\t */\n190\t\tmultiSelectFreeTextRowDetectLabels: readonly string[];\n191\t}\n192\t\n193\texport const PICKER_CONTRACTS: readonly PickerContract[] = [\n194\t\t{\n195\t\t\tversion: \"claude-code@2.1.220\",\n196\t\t\tfreeTextRowLabel: \"Other\",\n197\t\t\tfreeTextOneBytesProven: true,\n198\t\t\t// Never driven on this build. Not inferred from 2.1.226: the flags are per\n199\t\t\t// version precisely so a later build's proof cannot backfill an earlier one.\n200\t\t\tfreeTextManyBytesProven: false,\n201\t\t\tmultiSelectManyBytesProven: false,\n202\t\t\tmultiSelectFreeTextBytesProven: false,\n203\t\t\t// Never captured on 2.1.220 — check stays unarmed for multi items there.\n204\t\t\tmultiSelectFreeTextRowDetectLabels: [],\n205\t\t},\n206\t\t{\n207\t\t\tversion: \"claude-code@2.1.226\",\n208\t\t\t// Byte-exact, confirmed twice over: read out of the installed binary\n209\t\t\t// (`mL.multiSelect ? \"Type something\" : \"Type something.\"`) and then seen\n210\t\t\t// rendered in a live pty. The trailing full stop is real.\n211\t\t\tfreeTextRowLabel: \"Type something.\",\n212\t\t\t// DRIVEN, not inferred: digit N+1 opened the inline editor, the text was\n213\t\t\t// typed, `\\r` submitted, and the agent's own `tool_result` came back\n214\t\t\t// carrying that text verbatim. A `tool_result` is the only ground truth\n215\t\t\t// this contract accepts for a byte sequence.\n216\t\t\tfreeTextOneBytesProven: true,\n217\t\t\t// (FREETEXT-N2-PROVEN) Driven on N=2 prompts in three arrangements — free\n218\t\t\t// text on the first question, on the last, and on both — twice each, plus\n219\t\t\t// an N=3 prompt with free text in the middle. Every run's `tool_result`\n220\t\t\t// carried the typed text against the right question and the digit-selected\n221\t\t\t// label against the others.\n222\t\t\tfreeTextManyBytesProven: true,\n223\t\t\t// (MSEL-N2-PROVEN) Driven with the multi-select question FIRST (N=2),\n224\t\t\t// LAST (N=2) and in the MIDDLE (N=3), twice each, plus the empty\n225\t\t\t// zero-toggle group first and last — `tmp/pty-proof-msel/PROOF.md`. Every\n226\t\t\t// run's `tool_result` carried exactly the toggled options (a 3-option\n227\t\t\t// multi with `Mtwo` untouched proves no over-commit) and the digit answer\n228\t\t\t// on every sibling question. The ENTER probe in the same proof is why the\n229\t\t\t// encoder never emits `\\r` inside a multi-select group: it TOGGLED the\n230\t\t\t// caret's row (`Multi=\"Mthree, Mone\"` from one intended toggle).\n231\t\t\tmultiSelectManyBytesProven: true,\n232\t\t\t// REFUTED on this build, twice, and this is why it is a hard refusal\n233\t\t\t// rather than a cautious one. Pressing the multi-select free-text row's\n234\t\t\t// digit TOGGLES its checkbox and leaves the caret where it was; the body\n235\t\t\t// text is then swallowed entirely (nothing echoes); and the terminating\n236\t\t\t// `\\r` toggles whatever row the caret is still on. The run that drove it\n237\t\t\t// asked for free text and the agent's `tool_result` came back\n238\t\t\t// `\"…\"=\"Mone\"` — the first option, which nobody chose.\n239\t\t\tmultiSelectFreeTextBytesProven: false,\n240\t\t\t// (GUARD5-MSEL-EDITOR-DETECT) Captured live\n241\t\t\t// (tmp/pty-proof-msel/mix_ft_first-r1-04..06-*.txt): the checkbox editor\n242\t\t\t// row renders `[ ] Type something` untoggled — no full stop, unlike the\n243\t\t\t// single-select row above — and `[✔] Type something` once toggled.\n244\t\t\tmultiSelectFreeTextRowDetectLabels: [\n245\t\t\t\t\"[ ] Type something\",\n246\t\t\t\t\"[✔] Type something\",\n247\t\t\t],\n248\t\t},\n249\t];\n250\t\n251\texport function pickerContractFor(version: string): PickerContract | null {\n252\t\treturn (\n253\t\t\tPICKER_CONTRACTS.find((contract) => contract.version === version) ?? null\n254\t\t);\n255\t}\n256\t\n257\t/**\n258\t * The contract for the build the byte sequences are proven against. This is what\n259\t * the answer path drives, so it is what `question-store` derives the free-text\n260\t * row's label from.\n261\t */\n262\texport function provenPickerContract(): PickerContract | null {\n263\t\treturn pickerContractFor(PROVEN_AGAINST);\n264\t}\n265\t\n266\t/**\n267\t * (PICKER-CONTRACT-VERSIONED) The free-text row's label for the proven build, or\n268\t * `null` when that build has no proven free-text sequence / is unknown.\n269\t *\n270\t * THE label. Kept as its own accessor because the label is also what guard 5\n271\t * matches the row against and what the copy tests pin, but production code should\n272\t * reach for `provenFreeTextOption` instead: the label alone cannot say whether\n273\t * THIS prompt shape has a proven sequence.\n274\t */\n275\texport function provenFreeTextRowLabel(): string | null {\n276\t\tconst contract = provenPickerContract();\n277\t\tif (contract === null) return null;\n278\t\tif (!contract.freeTextOneBytesProven && !contract.freeTextManyBytesProven) {\n279\t\t\treturn null;\n280\t\t}\n281\t\treturn contract.freeTextRowLabel;\n282\t}\n283\t\n284\t/**\n285\t * (GUARD5-MSEL-EDITOR-DETECT) The multi-select editor row's exact raw-row\n286\t * copies for the proven build — one per toggle state — or `[]` when unknown.\n287\t *\n288\t * DETECTION ONLY. Guard 5 uses these as numbering evidence on multi-select\n289\t * items (the row renders below every option, so its presence proves missing\n290\t * option rows were not clipped away); nothing ever presses this row, and\n291\t * `provenFreeTextOption` keeps refusing multi-select free text regardless.\n292\t */\n293\texport function provenMultiSelectFreeTextDetectLabels(): readonly string[] {\n294\t\treturn provenPickerContract()?.multiSelectFreeTextRowDetectLabels ?? [];\n295\t}\n296\t\n297\t/**\n298\t * (FREETEXT-N2-PROVEN) THE free-text derivation — the whole of it, in one place.\n299\t *\n300\t * Both the capture PRODUCER\n301\t * (`trpc/router/notifications/companion-question-sink.ts`) and the capture\n302\t * VALIDATOR (`companion/question-store.ts`) call this. They previously held the\n303\t * shape rules AND the label independently, with a comment saying \"change the two\n304\t * together\" as the only thing holding them in agreement. When they drifted,\n305\t * `validateCapture` rejected EVERY single-question capture at ingestion — the\n306\t * hook 500s, the question is never stored and the phone is never notified — and\n307\t * no test caught it because they all pass `freeTextOption: null`, which skips the\n308\t * cross-check. So the rules moved here with the label, and the two call sites\n309\t * became one-line delegations that cannot disagree.\n310\t *\n311\t * Returns `null` — no free-text row offered, and `validateAnswerItem` then refuses\n312\t * any `freetext` answer for the item — whenever the SHAPE has no proven byte\n313\t * sequence on the proven build:\n314\t *\n315\t *   - `multiSelect`, on every build. On 2.1.226 this is a refutation, not\n316\t *     caution: the row is rendered, and driving it answers the question WRONG.\n317\t *     See `multiSelectFreeTextBytesProven`.\n318\t *   - `questionCount > 1` on a build where the per-question sequence was not\n319\t *     driven (2.1.220).\n320\t *   - an unknown build.\n321\t */\n322\texport function provenFreeTextOption(input: {\n323\t\tmultiSelect: boolean;\n324\t\toptionCount: number;\n325\t\tquestionCount: number;\n326\t}): { index: number; label: string } | null {\n327\t\tconst contract = provenPickerContract();\n328\t\tif (contract === null) return null;\n329\t\tif (input.multiSelect) {\n330\t\t\treturn contract.multiSelectFreeTextBytesProven\n331\t\t\t\t? { index: input.optionCount, label: contract.freeTextRowLabel }\n332\t\t\t\t: null;\n333\t\t}\n334\t\tconst proven =\n335\t\t\tinput.questionCount === 1\n336\t\t\t\t? contract.freeTextOneBytesProven\n337\t\t\t\t: contract.freeTextManyBytesProven;\n338\t\treturn proven\n339\t\t\t? { index: input.optionCount, label: contract.freeTextRowLabel }\n340\t\t\t: null;\n341\t}\n342\t\n343\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n344\t// raw bytes\n345\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n346\t\n347\t/**\n348\t * Right-arrow. On a LONE multi-select it moves onto the Submit tab; inside an\n349\t * N > 1 prompt it ADVANCES a multi-select question (next question mid-prompt,\n350\t * review screen on the last). Never a submit by itself.\n351\t */\n352\texport const KEY_RIGHT_ARROW = \"\\x1b[C\";\n353\t/** Carriage return. Submits a review screen / a free-text editor. TOGGLES a multi-select row. */\n354\texport const KEY_RETURN = \"\\r\";\n355\t\n356\t/** Highest option index that can be addressed by a single bare digit (\"9\"). */\n357\texport const MAX_DIGIT_ADDRESSABLE_INDEX = 8;\n358\t\n359\t/** PROTOCOL §11.2 — a free-text answer is 1..4 096 chars. */\n360\texport const FREETEXT_MAX_CHARS = 4_096;\n361\t\n362\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n363\t// errors\n364\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n365\t\n366\texport type KeystrokeEncodingReason =\n367\t\t/** The prompt/answer combination has no PROVEN byte sequence. Never guessed. */\n368\t\t| \"shape_unproven\"\n369\t\t/** `answers.length !== questions.length`, or indices are not 0..N-1 ascending. */"}]},"uuid":"8f5da7e1-edaf-4033-8ec8-5100034437d0","timestamp":"2026-08-12T11:13:42.643Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\keystrokes.ts","content":" * bridge that refused every answer the day after a Claude Code auto-update would\n * be a worse failure than the drift it was warning about.\n *\n * DO NOT advance this constant on the strength of reading the CLI's source. The\n * BYTES — that one bare digit still selects and submits, that digit N+1 opens the\n * editor, that `\\r` submits it — are behaviour, and only a live pty shows\n * behaviour. It stands at 2.1.226 because all three were DRIVEN there and the\n * agent's own `tool_result` confirmed each one; source reading alone would never\n * have earned it. `RENDER_OBSERVED_AGAINST` below is the weaker claim that source\n * reading does support, kept separate so nothing can quietly upgrade one into the\n * other.\n */\nexport const PROVEN_AGAINST = \"claude-code@2.1.226\";\n\n/**\n * The build whose RENDERED SHAPE has been observed directly: the free-text labels\n * below, and the row order `[...options, freeText, chat?]` with the chat row\n * carrying the sentinel `\"__chat__\"`.\n *\n * REFUTED by a live pty run, and corrected here rather than left as folklore: a\n * multi-select picker DOES render a free-text row at N+1 and the chat row at N+2.\n * The binary's `!mL.multiSelect` gate does not mean what the earlier comment\n * claimed. What that row does when its digit is pressed was then driven, twice,\n * and the answer is worse than \"unknown\" — see `multiSelectFreeTextBytesProven`.\n * Chat-row context-gating is still unconfirmed, so nothing asserts its presence\n * OR its absence.\n *\n * Strictly weaker than `PROVEN_AGAINST` and never a substitute for it. It exists\n * so the copy constants can cite what they are true of without implying the byte\n * contract was re-driven.\n */\nexport const RENDER_OBSERVED_AGAINST = \"claude-code@2.1.226\";\n\n/**\n * (PICKER-CONTRACT-VERSIONED) One entry per Claude Code build the fork has\n * OBSERVED, owning both halves of the contract for that build: the bytes, and the\n * exact copy the free-text row renders.\n *\n * The two halves have to live together and be keyed by version, because they were\n * briefly not: the free-text labels pinned 2.1.226's \"Type something.\" while\n * `PROVEN_AGAINST` declared 2.1.220, whose row reads \"Other\". One of the two was\n * always going to be false, and on the build whose bytes the fork claims to drive\n * the row would never have been found at all.\n *\n * The `*BytesProven` flags are PER SHAPE and per version, because the shapes are\n * genuinely independent behaviours of the same row: on 2.1.226 the one-question\n * and N>1 free-text sequences both work and the multi-select one is actively\n * harmful (see each flag). 2.1.220 is recorded as it was driven at the time —\n * only the one-question shape — and is NOT retro-fitted with claims about a build\n * nobody re-drove.\n *\n * An UNKNOWN version resolves to `null` and everything downstream fails closed.\n * To add a build: append an entry with what a pty run actually showed, and move\n * `PROVEN_AGAINST` only if its bytes were re-driven.\n */\nexport interface PickerContract {\n\tversion: string;\n\t/**\n\t * The label the free-text row renders, exactly, on a SINGLE-SELECT question.\n\t * A multi-select question renders different copy (2.1.226: \"Type something\",\n\t * no full stop) and a checkbox before it, which is deliberately not recorded\n\t * as a usable label — no multi-select free-text row is ever pressed.\n\t */\n\tfreeTextRowLabel: string;\n\t/**\n\t * Whether `[digit N+1, text, \"\\r\"]` was driven on a prompt of exactly ONE\n\t * single-select question, where the `\\r` submits the whole prompt.\n\t */\n\tfreeTextOneBytesProven: boolean;\n\t/**\n\t * (FREETEXT-N2-PROVEN) Whether the SAME three keystrokes were driven for a\n\t * question inside an N > 1 single-select prompt, where the `\\r` advances to\n\t * the next question and the prompt is submitted by the review-screen return.\n\t */\n\tfreeTextManyBytesProven: boolean;\n\t/**\n\t * (MSEL-N2-PROVEN) Whether `[toggle digit × k, \"\\x1b[C\"]` was driven as a\n\t * per-question group for a multi-select question INSIDE an N > 1 prompt —\n\t * the arrow advancing to the next question mid-prompt and onto the review\n\t * screen on the last, with the review-screen `\\r` submitting.\n\t */\n\tmultiSelectManyBytesProven: boolean;\n\t/**\n\t * Whether free text on a MULTI-SELECT question has a proven sequence.\n\t *\n\t * `false` on every build, and on 2.1.226 that is a REFUTATION rather than an\n\t * absence of evidence — see the refusal in `validateAnswerItem`.\n\t */\n\tmultiSelectFreeTextBytesProven: boolean;\n\t/**\n\t * (GUARD5-MSEL-EDITOR-DETECT) DETECTION-ONLY: the exact raw-row copy of the\n\t * multi-select editor row in each toggle state, as the build renders it at\n\t * digit `options.length + 1`. Guard 5 uses it as numbering evidence — the\n\t * row renders BELOW every option, so seeing it while options are missing\n\t * proves a mis-numbered screen rather than a clip. It is NEVER offered as a\n\t * pressable slot (`provenFreeTextOption` refuses multi-select free text;\n\t * on 2.1.226 the press is refuted, not unproven). Empty when the copy was\n\t * never captured for the build — the contradiction check then simply stays\n\t * unarmed for multi items, exactly as it was before the needle existed.\n\t */\n\tmultiSelectFreeTextRowDetectLabels: readonly string[];\n}\n\nexport const PICKER_CONTRACTS: readonly PickerContract[] = [\n\t{\n\t\tversion: \"claude-code@2.1.220\",\n\t\tfreeTextRowLabel: \"Other\",\n\t\tfreeTextOneBytesProven: true,\n\t\t// Never driven on this build. Not inferred from 2.1.226: the flags are per\n\t\t// version precisely so a later build's proof cannot backfill an earlier one.\n\t\tfreeTextManyBytesProven: false,\n\t\tmultiSelectManyBytesProven: false,\n\t\tmultiSelectFreeTextBytesProven: false,\n\t\t// Never captured on 2.1.220 — check stays unarmed for multi items there.\n\t\tmultiSelectFreeTextRowDetectLabels: [],\n\t},\n\t{\n\t\tversion: \"claude-code@2.1.226\",\n\t\t// Byte-exact, confirmed twice over: read out of the installed binary\n\t\t// (`mL.multiSelect ? \"Type something\" : \"Type something.\"`) and then seen\n\t\t// rendered in a live pty. The trailing full stop is real.\n\t\tfreeTextRowLabel: \"Type something.\",\n\t\t// DRIVEN, not inferred: digit N+1 opened the inline editor, the text was\n\t\t// typed, `\\r` submitted, and the agent's own `tool_result` came back\n\t\t// carrying that text verbatim. A `tool_result` is the only ground truth\n\t\t// this contract accepts for a byte sequence.\n\t\tfreeTextOneBytesProven: true,\n\t\t// (FREETEXT-N2-PROVEN) Driven on N=2 prompts in three arrangements — free\n\t\t// text on the first question, on the last, and on both — twice each, plus\n\t\t// an N=3 prompt with free text in the middle. Every run's `tool_result`\n\t\t// carried the typed text against the right question and the digit-selected\n\t\t// label against the others.\n\t\tfreeTextManyBytesProven: true,\n\t\t// (MSEL-N2-PROVEN) Driven with the multi-select question FIRST (N=2),\n\t\t// LAST (N=2) and in the MIDDLE (N=3), twice each, plus the empty\n\t\t// zero-toggle group first and last — `tmp/pty-proof-msel/PROOF.md`. Every\n\t\t// run's `tool_result` carried exactly the toggled options (a 3-option\n\t\t// multi with `Mtwo` untouched proves no over-commit) and the digit answer\n\t\t// on every sibling question. The ENTER probe in the same proof is why the\n\t\t// encoder never emits `\\r` inside a multi-select group: it TOGGLED the\n\t\t// caret's row (`Multi=\"Mthree, Mone\"` from one intended toggle).\n\t\tmultiSelectManyBytesProven: true,\n\t\t// REFUTED on this build, twice, and this is why it is a hard refusal\n\t\t// rather than a cautious one. Pressing the multi-select free-text row's\n\t\t// digit TOGGLES its checkbox and leaves the caret where it was; the body\n\t\t// text is then swallowed entirely (nothing echoes); and the terminating\n\t\t// `\\r` toggles whatever row the caret is still on. The run that drove it\n\t\t// asked for free text and the agent's `tool_result` came back\n\t\t// `\"…\"=\"Mone\"` — the first option, which nobody chose.\n\t\tmultiSelectFreeTextBytesProven: false,\n\t\t// (GUARD5-MSEL-EDITOR-DETECT) Captured live\n\t\t// (tmp/pty-proof-msel/mix_ft_first-r1-04..06-*.txt): the checkbox editor\n\t\t// row renders `[ ] Type something` untoggled — no full stop, unlike the\n\t\t// single-select row above — and `[✔] Type something` once toggled.\n\t\tmultiSelectFreeTextRowDetectLabels: [\n\t\t\t\"[ ] Type something\",\n\t\t\t\"[✔] Type something\",\n\t\t],\n\t},\n];\n\nexport function pickerContractFor(version: string): PickerContract | null {\n\treturn (\n\t\tPICKER_CONTRACTS.find((contract) => contract.version === version) ?? null\n\t);\n}\n\n/**\n * The contract for the build the byte sequences are proven against. This is what\n * the answer path drives, so it is what `question-store` derives the free-text\n * row's label from.\n */\nexport function provenPickerContract(): PickerContract | null {\n\treturn pickerContractFor(PROVEN_AGAINST);\n}\n\n/**\n * (PICKER-CONTRACT-VERSIONED) The free-text row's label for the proven build, or\n * `null` when that build has no proven free-text sequence / is unknown.\n *\n * THE label. Kept as its own accessor because the label is also what guard 5\n * matches the row against and what the copy tests pin, but production code should\n * reach for `provenFreeTextOption` instead: the label alone cannot say whether\n * THIS prompt shape has a proven sequence.\n */\nexport function provenFreeTextRowLabel(): string | null {\n\tconst contract = provenPickerContract();\n\tif (contract === null) return null;\n\tif (!contract.freeTextOneBytesProven && !contract.freeTextManyBytesProven) {\n\t\treturn null;\n\t}\n\treturn contract.freeTextRowLabel;\n}\n\n/**\n * (GUARD5-MSEL-EDITOR-DETECT) The multi-select editor row's exact raw-row\n * copies for the proven build — one per toggle state — or `[]` when unknown.\n *\n * DETECTION ONLY. Guard 5 uses these as numbering evidence on multi-select\n * items (the row renders below every option, so its presence proves missing\n * option rows were not clipped away); nothing ever presses this row, and\n * `provenFreeTextOption` keeps refusing multi-select free text regardless.\n */\nexport function provenMultiSelectFreeTextDetectLabels(): readonly string[] {\n\treturn provenPickerContract()?.multiSelectFreeTextRowDetectLabels ?? [];\n}\n\n/**\n * (FREETEXT-N2-PROVEN) THE free-text derivation — the whole of it, in one place.\n *\n * Both the capture PRODUCER\n * (`trpc/router/notifications/companion-question-sink.ts`) and the capture\n * VALIDATOR (`companion/question-store.ts`) call this. They previously held the\n * shape rules AND the label independently, with a comment saying \"change the two\n * together\" as the only thing holding them in agreement. When they drifted,\n * `validateCapture` rejected EVERY single-question capture at ingestion — the\n * hook 500s, the question is never stored and the phone is never notified — and\n * no test caught it because they all pass `freeTextOption: null`, which skips the\n * cross-check. So the rules moved here with the label, and the two call sites\n * became one-line delegations that cannot disagree.\n *\n * Returns `null` — no free-text row offered, and `validateAnswerItem` then refuses\n * any `freetext` answer for the item — whenever the SHAPE has no proven byte\n * sequence on the proven build:\n *\n *   - `multiSelect`, on every build. On 2.1.226 this is a refutation, not\n *     caution: the row is rendered, and driving it answers the question WRONG.\n *     See `multiSelectFreeTextBytesProven`.\n *   - `questionCount > 1` on a build where the per-question sequence was not\n *     driven (2.1.220).\n *   - an unknown build.\n */\nexport function provenFreeTextOption(input: {\n\tmultiSelect: boolean;\n\toptionCount: number;\n\tquestionCount: number;\n}): { index: number; label: string } | null {\n\tconst contract = provenPickerContract();\n\tif (contract === null) return null;\n\tif (input.multiSelect) {\n\t\treturn contract.multiSelectFreeTextBytesProven\n\t\t\t? { index: input.optionCount, label: contract.freeTextRowLabel }\n\t\t\t: null;\n\t}\n\tconst proven =\n\t\tinput.questionCount === 1\n\t\t\t? contract.freeTextOneBytesProven\n\t\t\t: contract.freeTextManyBytesProven;\n\treturn proven\n\t\t? { index: input.optionCount, label: contract.freeTextRowLabel }\n\t\t: null;\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// raw bytes\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n/**\n * Right-arrow. On a LONE multi-select it moves onto the Submit tab; inside an\n * N > 1 prompt it ADVANCES a multi-select question (next question mid-prompt,\n * review screen on the last). Never a submit by itself.\n */\nexport const KEY_RIGHT_ARROW = \"\\x1b[C\";\n/** Carriage return. Submits a review screen / a free-text editor. TOGGLES a multi-select row. */\nexport const KEY_RETURN = \"\\r\";\n\n/** Highest option index that can be addressed by a single bare digit (\"9\"). */\nexport const MAX_DIGIT_ADDRESSABLE_INDEX = 8;\n\n/** PROTOCOL §11.2 — a free-text answer is 1..4 096 chars. */\nexport const FREETEXT_MAX_CHARS = 4_096;\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// errors\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nexport type KeystrokeEncodingReason =\n\t/** The prompt/answer combination has no PROVEN byte sequence. Never guessed. */\n\t| \"shape_unproven\"\n\t/** `answers.length !== questions.length`, or indices are not 0..N-1 ascending. */","numLines":280,"startLine":90,"totalLines":1783}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"dc96910b-06aa-46fe-a530-4d69f0c98e3b","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
{"parentUuid":"8f5da7e1-edaf-4033-8ec8-5100034437d0","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"resp_035a17cc89f6159b016a7c55694abc81918d1ebc052d3bef83","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"codex-gpt-5-6-sol","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4694,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":104960,"output_tokens":58,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_aw28cUSxONEPJpEBHuIpObn0","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\keystrokes.ts","offset":420,"limit":240}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_details":null},"attributionSkill":"windows-computer-use","type":"assistant","uuid":"07e5f58a-8085-4d00-87cb-491579112d84","timestamp":"2026-08-12T11:13:48.436Z","effort":"high","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
{"parentUuid":"07e5f58a-8085-4d00-87cb-491579112d84","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"44611460-2829-462e-b2e9-af8e653be0db","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_aw28cUSxONEPJpEBHuIpObn0","type":"tool_result","content":"420\t * ADVANCE — was driven first, middle and last (`tmp/pty-proof-msel/PROOF.md`).\n421\t * The earlier refutation of this shape refuted only the N=1 submit pair\n422\t * (`\\x1b[C` then `\\r`) reused verbatim: that trailing `\\r` landed on the NEXT\n423\t * question and selected its focused row. The group form has no trailing `\\r`,\n424\t * and `assertNoReturnIntoMultiSelect` makes emitting one impossible.\n425\t *\n426\t * Still deliberately absent: free text ON a multi-select question — REFUTED,\n427\t * not unproven (see `multiSelectFreeTextBytesProven`).\n428\t */\n429\texport type AnswerShape =\n430\t\t/** Exactly one single-select question: `[digit]`. Atomic select+submit. */\n431\t\t| \"single_select_one\"\n432\t\t/** N > 1 questions, ALL single-select, ALL answered by digit: `[digit × N, \"\\r\"]`. */\n433\t\t| \"single_select_many\"\n434\t\t/** Exactly one multi-select question: `[toggle × k, \"\\x1b[C\", \"\\r\"]`. */\n435\t\t| \"multiselect_one\"\n436\t\t/** Exactly one question answered with free text: `[digit N+1, text, \"\\r\"]`. */\n437\t\t| \"freetext_one\"\n438\t\t/**\n439\t\t * (FREETEXT-N2-PROVEN) N > 1 single-select questions, at least one answered\n440\t\t * with free text: per question either `[digit]` or `[digit N+1, text, \"\\r\"]`,\n441\t\t * then the review-screen `\"\\r\"`.\n442\t\t */\n443\t\t| \"freetext_many\"\n444\t\t/**\n445\t\t * (MSEL-N2-PROVEN) N > 1 questions, at least one multi-select. Per question\n446\t\t * either `[digit]`, `[digit N+1, text, \"\\r\"]` (free text on a single-select\n447\t\t * sibling), or `[toggle digit × k, \"\\x1b[C\"]` for a multi-select — k may be\n448\t\t * 0, which the CLI accepts and then OMITS that question from the\n449\t\t * `tool_result`, exactly as a desk submit past the review-screen warning\n450\t\t * does. Then the review-screen `\"\\r\"`.\n451\t\t */\n452\t\t| \"multiselect_many\";\n453\t\n454\texport type KeystrokeKind =\n455\t\t/** A bare digit that selects (and, when it is the only one, submits). */\n456\t\t| \"select_digit\"\n457\t\t/** A bare digit that TOGGLES a multi-select row. */\n458\t\t| \"toggle_digit\"\n459\t\t/** The digit that opens the inline free-text editor. */\n460\t\t| \"freetext_open\"\n461\t\t/** The UTF-8 free-text body. */\n462\t\t| \"freetext_body\"\n463\t\t/**\n464\t\t * Right-arrow. On a lone multi-select it moves onto the Submit tab; inside\n465\t\t * an N > 1 prompt it ADVANCES a multi-select question (next question\n466\t\t * mid-prompt, review screen on the last). The same byte either way, and\n467\t\t * never itself a submit. The picker's own footer under the rows reads\n468\t\t * `Next` mid-prompt and `Submit` on the last question — a rendering detail,\n469\t\t * never a reason to branch on which byte to send.\n470\t\t */\n471\t\t| \"submit_tab\"\n472\t\t/** `\\r` — submits a review screen or a free-text editor. */\n473\t\t| \"submit_return\";\n474\t\n475\t/**\n476\t * What the picker MUST be showing immediately before a keystroke is written.\n477\t * Guard 5 is re-evaluated against this before EVERY keystroke, because each one\n478\t * lands on a screen the previous one changed.\n479\t */\n480\texport type ScreenExpectation =\n481\t\t/**\n482\t\t * The numbered option list for `itemIndex` is on screen, and the digit this\n483\t\t * keystroke is about to press maps to the option we think it does. This is\n484\t\t * the STRONG form and it is what makes guard 5 load-bearing.\n485\t\t */\n486\t\t| { kind: \"item_picker\"; itemIndex: number }\n487\t\t/**\n488\t\t * A screen whose exact shape was never proven (the N-question review screen,\n489\t\t * the multi-select Submit tab, the open free-text editor). The only sound\n490\t\t * assertion available is the WEAK one: this prompt is still the thing on\n491\t\t * screen, anchored on `itemIndex`'s header. Weaker than `item_picker` and\n492\t\t * documented as such — never presented as equivalent.\n493\t\t */\n494\t\t| { kind: \"same_prompt\"; itemIndex: number };\n495\t\n496\texport interface Keystroke {\n497\t\tkind: KeystrokeKind;\n498\t\t/** The exact bytes, written verbatim by the raw pty writer. Never reframed. */\n499\t\tdata: string;\n500\t\t/** 0-based index of the `QuestionItem` this keystroke acts on. */\n501\t\tquestionIndex: number;\n502\t\t/** The option index this keystroke selects/toggles/opens, when applicable. */\n503\t\toptionIndex: number | null;\n504\t\t/** Re-checked by guard 5 immediately before this keystroke is written. */\n505\t\texpect: ScreenExpectation;\n506\t\t/**\n507\t\t * true => after this keystroke the prompt is submitted and the picker is\n508\t\t * gone. Exactly one keystroke in a sequence carries this.\n509\t\t */\n510\t\tsubmits: boolean;\n511\t}\n512\t\n513\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n514\t// §11.2 boundary validation\n515\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n516\t\n517\t/**\n518\t * PROTOCOL §11.2 — validate at the API boundary, before anything else happens.\n519\t * Every failure is a hard error; nothing is coerced, normalised or defaulted.\n520\t *\n521\t * In particular a duplicate in `optionIndexes` is REFUSED rather than\n522\t * de-duplicated: two toggles of the same row cancel out, so \"normalising\" it\n523\t * would be guessing at intent and would silently answer something the user did\n524\t * not choose.\n525\t */\n526\texport function validateAnswerItems(\n527\t\tquestions: readonly QuestionItem[],\n528\t\tanswers: readonly AnswerItem[],\n529\t): void {\n530\t\tif (questions.length === 0) {\n531\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\"arity_mismatch\", \"question has no items\");\n532\t\t}\n533\t\tif (answers.length !== questions.length) {\n534\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n535\t\t\t\t\"arity_mismatch\",\n536\t\t\t\t`answers.length ${answers.length} !== questions.length ${questions.length}; a partial answer is not a thing`,\n537\t\t\t);\n538\t\t}\n539\t\n540\t\tfor (let i = 0; i < answers.length; i += 1) {\n541\t\t\tconst answer = answers[i];\n542\t\t\tconst item = questions[i];\n543\t\t\tif (answer === undefined || item === undefined) {\n544\t\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n545\t\t\t\t\t\"arity_mismatch\",\n546\t\t\t\t\t`missing answer or question item at position ${i}`,\n547\t\t\t\t);\n548\t\t\t}\n549\t\t\tif (answer.questionIndex !== i) {\n550\t\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n551\t\t\t\t\t\"arity_mismatch\",\n552\t\t\t\t\t`answers must be 0..N-1, each once, ascending; position ${i} carries questionIndex ${answer.questionIndex}`,\n553\t\t\t\t);\n554\t\t\t}\n555\t\t\tif (item.index !== i) {\n556\t\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n557\t\t\t\t\t\"arity_mismatch\",\n558\t\t\t\t\t`question item at position ${i} declares index ${item.index}`,\n559\t\t\t\t);\n560\t\t\t}\n561\t\t\tvalidateAnswerItem(item, answer);\n562\t\t}\n563\t}\n564\t\n565\tfunction validateAnswerItem(item: QuestionItem, answer: AnswerItem): void {\n566\t\tconst optionCount = item.options.length;\n567\t\n568\t\tswitch (answer.kind) {\n569\t\t\tcase \"select\": {\n570\t\t\t\tif (item.multiSelect) {\n571\t\t\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n572\t\t\t\t\t\t\"kind_mismatch\",\n573\t\t\t\t\t\t`item ${item.index} is multiSelect; \"select\" is never coerced into it`,\n574\t\t\t\t\t);\n575\t\t\t\t}\n576\t\t\t\tassertOptionIndex(answer.optionIndex, optionCount, item.index);\n577\t\t\t\treturn;\n578\t\t\t}\n579\t\t\tcase \"multiselect\": {\n580\t\t\t\tif (!item.multiSelect) {\n581\t\t\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n582\t\t\t\t\t\t\"kind_mismatch\",\n583\t\t\t\t\t\t`item ${item.index} is single-select; \"multiselect\" is never coerced into it`,\n584\t\t\t\t\t);\n585\t\t\t\t}\n586\t\t\t\tif (answer.optionIndexes.length === 0) {\n587\t\t\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n588\t\t\t\t\t\t\"empty_selection\",\n589\t\t\t\t\t\t`item ${item.index} selects nothing; the desk CLI silently drops an untoggled multi-select question from the agent's result, so an empty selection cannot be committed`,\n590\t\t\t\t\t);\n591\t\t\t\t}\n592\t\t\t\tlet previous = -1;\n593\t\t\t\tfor (const optionIndex of answer.optionIndexes) {\n594\t\t\t\t\tassertOptionIndex(optionIndex, optionCount, item.index);\n595\t\t\t\t\tif (optionIndex === previous) {\n596\t\t\t\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n597\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\"duplicate_option\",\n598\t\t\t\t\t\t\t`item ${item.index} repeats option ${optionIndex}; two toggles cancel out, so this is refused rather than normalised`,\n599\t\t\t\t\t\t);\n600\t\t\t\t\t}\n601\t\t\t\t\tif (optionIndex < previous) {\n602\t\t\t\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n603\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\"unsorted_options\",\n604\t\t\t\t\t\t\t`item ${item.index} optionIndexes must be sorted ascending`,\n605\t\t\t\t\t\t);\n606\t\t\t\t\t}\n607\t\t\t\t\tprevious = optionIndex;\n608\t\t\t\t}\n609\t\t\t\treturn;\n610\t\t\t}\n611\t\t\tcase \"freetext\": {\n612\t\t\t\t// PROTOCOL §11.3: `kind` MUST match the item's `multiSelect` flag AND\n613\t\t\t\t// the presence of `freeTextOption`. Both conjuncts are checked, and the\n614\t\t\t\t// multiSelect one first, because on 2.1.226 driving that row is not\n615\t\t\t\t// merely unproven — it was DRIVEN and it answers the question wrong.\n616\t\t\t\t// Twice: the digit toggles the row's checkbox without moving the caret,\n617\t\t\t\t// the body text is swallowed with no echo at all, and the terminating\n618\t\t\t\t// `\\r` toggles whatever row the caret was still resting on. The run that\n619\t\t\t\t// asked for free text got back `tool_result` `\"…\"=\"Mone\"`, the first\n620\t\t\t\t// option. Symmetric with the \"select\" and \"multiselect\" cases above; a\n621\t\t\t\t// producer that synthesised a free-text slot onto a multiSelect item\n622\t\t\t\t// must be refused here, not merely be unreachable through a well-behaved\n623\t\t\t\t// client.\n624\t\t\t\tif (item.multiSelect) {\n625\t\t\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n626\t\t\t\t\t\t\"kind_mismatch\",\n627\t\t\t\t\t\t`item ${item.index} is multiSelect; its free-text row toggles a checkbox and swallows the text, so \"freetext\" is never coerced into it`,\n628\t\t\t\t\t);\n629\t\t\t\t}\n630\t\t\t\tif (item.freeTextOption === null) {\n631\t\t\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n632\t\t\t\t\t\t\"kind_mismatch\",\n633\t\t\t\t\t\t`item ${item.index} has no free-text slot`,\n634\t\t\t\t\t);\n635\t\t\t\t}\n636\t\t\t\tif (item.freeTextOption.index !== optionCount) {\n637\t\t\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n638\t\t\t\t\t\t\"option_out_of_range\",\n639\t\t\t\t\t\t`item ${item.index} free-text slot is ${item.freeTextOption.index}, expected ${optionCount}`,\n640\t\t\t\t\t);\n641\t\t\t\t}\n642\t\t\t\tassertFreeText(answer.text, item.index);\n643\t\t\t\treturn;\n644\t\t\t}\n645\t\t\tdefault: {\n646\t\t\t\t// Exhaustive: `AnswerItem` is a closed union. An unknown kind reaching\n647\t\t\t\t// here is a decoder bug, and is surfaced rather than ignored.\n648\t\t\t\tconst unreachable: never = answer;\n649\t\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n650\t\t\t\t\t\"kind_mismatch\",\n651\t\t\t\t\t`unknown answer kind: ${JSON.stringify(unreachable)}`,\n652\t\t\t\t);\n653\t\t\t}\n654\t\t}\n655\t}\n656\t\n657\tfunction assertOptionIndex(\n658\t\toptionIndex: number,\n659\t\toptionCount: number,"}]},"uuid":"a3051889-ee24-4e9b-aca2-bb5ce9d9ceb0","timestamp":"2026-08-12T11:13:48.792Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\keystrokes.ts","content":" * ADVANCE — was driven first, middle and last (`tmp/pty-proof-msel/PROOF.md`).\n * The earlier refutation of this shape refuted only the N=1 submit pair\n * (`\\x1b[C` then `\\r`) reused verbatim: that trailing `\\r` landed on the NEXT\n * question and selected its focused row. The group form has no trailing `\\r`,\n * and `assertNoReturnIntoMultiSelect` makes emitting one impossible.\n *\n * Still deliberately absent: free text ON a multi-select question — REFUTED,\n * not unproven (see `multiSelectFreeTextBytesProven`).\n */\nexport type AnswerShape =\n\t/** Exactly one single-select question: `[digit]`. Atomic select+submit. */\n\t| \"single_select_one\"\n\t/** N > 1 questions, ALL single-select, ALL answered by digit: `[digit × N, \"\\r\"]`. */\n\t| \"single_select_many\"\n\t/** Exactly one multi-select question: `[toggle × k, \"\\x1b[C\", \"\\r\"]`. */\n\t| \"multiselect_one\"\n\t/** Exactly one question answered with free text: `[digit N+1, text, \"\\r\"]`. */\n\t| \"freetext_one\"\n\t/**\n\t * (FREETEXT-N2-PROVEN) N > 1 single-select questions, at least one answered\n\t * with free text: per question either `[digit]` or `[digit N+1, text, \"\\r\"]`,\n\t * then the review-screen `\"\\r\"`.\n\t */\n\t| \"freetext_many\"\n\t/**\n\t * (MSEL-N2-PROVEN) N > 1 questions, at least one multi-select. Per question\n\t * either `[digit]`, `[digit N+1, text, \"\\r\"]` (free text on a single-select\n\t * sibling), or `[toggle digit × k, \"\\x1b[C\"]` for a multi-select — k may be\n\t * 0, which the CLI accepts and then OMITS that question from the\n\t * `tool_result`, exactly as a desk submit past the review-screen warning\n\t * does. Then the review-screen `\"\\r\"`.\n\t */\n\t| \"multiselect_many\";\n\nexport type KeystrokeKind =\n\t/** A bare digit that selects (and, when it is the only one, submits). */\n\t| \"select_digit\"\n\t/** A bare digit that TOGGLES a multi-select row. */\n\t| \"toggle_digit\"\n\t/** The digit that opens the inline free-text editor. */\n\t| \"freetext_open\"\n\t/** The UTF-8 free-text body. */\n\t| \"freetext_body\"\n\t/**\n\t * Right-arrow. On a lone multi-select it moves onto the Submit tab; inside\n\t * an N > 1 prompt it ADVANCES a multi-select question (next question\n\t * mid-prompt, review screen on the last). The same byte either way, and\n\t * never itself a submit. The picker's own footer under the rows reads\n\t * `Next` mid-prompt and `Submit` on the last question — a rendering detail,\n\t * never a reason to branch on which byte to send.\n\t */\n\t| \"submit_tab\"\n\t/** `\\r` — submits a review screen or a free-text editor. */\n\t| \"submit_return\";\n\n/**\n * What the picker MUST be showing immediately before a keystroke is written.\n * Guard 5 is re-evaluated against this before EVERY keystroke, because each one\n * lands on a screen the previous one changed.\n */\nexport type ScreenExpectation =\n\t/**\n\t * The numbered option list for `itemIndex` is on screen, and the digit this\n\t * keystroke is about to press maps to the option we think it does. This is\n\t * the STRONG form and it is what makes guard 5 load-bearing.\n\t */\n\t| { kind: \"item_picker\"; itemIndex: number }\n\t/**\n\t * A screen whose exact shape was never proven (the N-question review screen,\n\t * the multi-select Submit tab, the open free-text editor). The only sound\n\t * assertion available is the WEAK one: this prompt is still the thing on\n\t * screen, anchored on `itemIndex`'s header. Weaker than `item_picker` and\n\t * documented as such — never presented as equivalent.\n\t */\n\t| { kind: \"same_prompt\"; itemIndex: number };\n\nexport interface Keystroke {\n\tkind: KeystrokeKind;\n\t/** The exact bytes, written verbatim by the raw pty writer. Never reframed. */\n\tdata: string;\n\t/** 0-based index of the `QuestionItem` this keystroke acts on. */\n\tquestionIndex: number;\n\t/** The option index this keystroke selects/toggles/opens, when applicable. */\n\toptionIndex: number | null;\n\t/** Re-checked by guard 5 immediately before this keystroke is written. */\n\texpect: ScreenExpectation;\n\t/**\n\t * true => after this keystroke the prompt is submitted and the picker is\n\t * gone. Exactly one keystroke in a sequence carries this.\n\t */\n\tsubmits: boolean;\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// §11.2 boundary validation\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n/**\n * PROTOCOL §11.2 — validate at the API boundary, before anything else happens.\n * Every failure is a hard error; nothing is coerced, normalised or defaulted.\n *\n * In particular a duplicate in `optionIndexes` is REFUSED rather than\n * de-duplicated: two toggles of the same row cancel out, so \"normalising\" it\n * would be guessing at intent and would silently answer something the user did\n * not choose.\n */\nexport function validateAnswerItems(\n\tquestions: readonly QuestionItem[],\n\tanswers: readonly AnswerItem[],\n): void {\n\tif (questions.length === 0) {\n\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\"arity_mismatch\", \"question has no items\");\n\t}\n\tif (answers.length !== questions.length) {\n\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n\t\t\t\"arity_mismatch\",\n\t\t\t`answers.length ${answers.length} !== questions.length ${questions.length}; a partial answer is not a thing`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\n\tfor (let i = 0; i < answers.length; i += 1) {\n\t\tconst answer = answers[i];\n\t\tconst item = questions[i];\n\t\tif (answer === undefined || item === undefined) {\n\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n\t\t\t\t\"arity_mismatch\",\n\t\t\t\t`missing answer or question item at position ${i}`,\n\t\t\t);\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (answer.questionIndex !== i) {\n\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n\t\t\t\t\"arity_mismatch\",\n\t\t\t\t`answers must be 0..N-1, each once, ascending; position ${i} carries questionIndex ${answer.questionIndex}`,\n\t\t\t);\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (item.index !== i) {\n\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n\t\t\t\t\"arity_mismatch\",\n\t\t\t\t`question item at position ${i} declares index ${item.index}`,\n\t\t\t);\n\t\t}\n\t\tvalidateAnswerItem(item, answer);\n\t}\n}\n\nfunction validateAnswerItem(item: QuestionItem, answer: AnswerItem): void {\n\tconst optionCount = item.options.length;\n\n\tswitch (answer.kind) {\n\t\tcase \"select\": {\n\t\t\tif (item.multiSelect) {\n\t\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n\t\t\t\t\t\"kind_mismatch\",\n\t\t\t\t\t`item ${item.index} is multiSelect; \"select\" is never coerced into it`,\n\t\t\t\t);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tassertOptionIndex(answer.optionIndex, optionCount, item.index);\n\t\t\treturn;\n\t\t}\n\t\tcase \"multiselect\": {\n\t\t\tif (!item.multiSelect) {\n\t\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n\t\t\t\t\t\"kind_mismatch\",\n\t\t\t\t\t`item ${item.index} is single-select; \"multiselect\" is never coerced into it`,\n\t\t\t\t);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif (answer.optionIndexes.length === 0) {\n\t\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n\t\t\t\t\t\"empty_selection\",\n\t\t\t\t\t`item ${item.index} selects nothing; the desk CLI silently drops an untoggled multi-select question from the agent's result, so an empty selection cannot be committed`,\n\t\t\t\t);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tlet previous = -1;\n\t\t\tfor (const optionIndex of answer.optionIndexes) {\n\t\t\t\tassertOptionIndex(optionIndex, optionCount, item.index);\n\t\t\t\tif (optionIndex === previous) {\n\t\t\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\"duplicate_option\",\n\t\t\t\t\t\t`item ${item.index} repeats option ${optionIndex}; two toggles cancel out, so this is refused rather than normalised`,\n\t\t\t\t\t);\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\tif (optionIndex < previous) {\n\t\t\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\"unsorted_options\",\n\t\t\t\t\t\t`item ${item.index} optionIndexes must be sorted ascending`,\n\t\t\t\t\t);\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\tprevious = optionIndex;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\treturn;\n\t\t}\n\t\tcase \"freetext\": {\n\t\t\t// PROTOCOL §11.3: `kind` MUST match the item's `multiSelect` flag AND\n\t\t\t// the presence of `freeTextOption`. Both conjuncts are checked, and the\n\t\t\t// multiSelect one first, because on 2.1.226 driving that row is not\n\t\t\t// merely unproven — it was DRIVEN and it answers the question wrong.\n\t\t\t// Twice: the digit toggles the row's checkbox without moving the caret,\n\t\t\t// the body text is swallowed with no echo at all, and the terminating\n\t\t\t// `\\r` toggles whatever row the caret was still resting on. The run that\n\t\t\t// asked for free text got back `tool_result` `\"…\"=\"Mone\"`, the first\n\t\t\t// option. Symmetric with the \"select\" and \"multiselect\" cases above; a\n\t\t\t// producer that synthesised a free-text slot onto a multiSelect item\n\t\t\t// must be refused here, not merely be unreachable through a well-behaved\n\t\t\t// client.\n\t\t\tif (item.multiSelect) {\n\t\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n\t\t\t\t\t\"kind_mismatch\",\n\t\t\t\t\t`item ${item.index} is multiSelect; its free-text row toggles a checkbox and swallows the text, so \"freetext\" is never coerced into it`,\n\t\t\t\t);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif (item.freeTextOption === null) {\n\t\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n\t\t\t\t\t\"kind_mismatch\",\n\t\t\t\t\t`item ${item.index} has no free-text slot`,\n\t\t\t\t);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif (item.freeTextOption.index !== optionCount) {\n\t\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n\t\t\t\t\t\"option_out_of_range\",\n\t\t\t\t\t`item ${item.index} free-text slot is ${item.freeTextOption.index}, expected ${optionCount}`,\n\t\t\t\t);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tassertFreeText(answer.text, item.index);\n\t\t\treturn;\n\t\t}\n\t\tdefault: {\n\t\t\t// Exhaustive: `AnswerItem` is a closed union. An unknown kind reaching\n\t\t\t// here is a decoder bug, and is surfaced rather than ignored.\n\t\t\tconst unreachable: never = answer;\n\t\t\tthrow new KeystrokeEncodingError(\n\t\t\t\t\"kind_mismatch\",\n\t\t\t\t`unknown answer kind: ${JSON.stringify(unreachable)}`,\n\t\t\t);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n\nfunction assertOptionIndex(\n\toptionIndex: number,\n\toptionCount: number,","numLines":240,"startLine":420,"totalLines":1783}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"07e5f58a-8085-4d00-87cb-4915791